      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to evaluate the efficacy of uterine artery embolisation (UAE) in myomatous uteri larger than 24 week's gestation (780 cm3)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective case control study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Universitas Hospital, University of the Free State, Bloemfontein, South Africa."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "POPULATION",
          "content": "Sixty-one women, who underwent UAE, were included in the study. The study group comprised of 12 women with uteri > or =780 cm3 and the control group 49 women with uteri <780 cm3."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "UAE was performed and the difference in outcome for the two groups was determined at 12 months."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE",
          "content": "Symptomatic improvement with embolisation of the large uterus."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Reduction of dysmenorrhoea, menorrhagia and pressure effects was similar for both groups. The median reduction in uterine volume (pre- to post-embolisation) was 188 cm3 (range 28-2038 cm3) with a 95% CI for the median difference for paired data of 146.5 and 236. Only 66% of the study group had, however, a reduction in volume to <780 cm3. The complication rates were similar for the two groups with regards to post-embolisation syndrome, fibroid slough, haematoma formation, infection, hysterectomy and failure to embolise. Satisfaction was similar between the two groups, with 91% of women satisfied with the procedure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The large uterus does not decrease UAE's efficacy. Although 33.3% of the study group still had a uterus of > or =780 cm3, symptom reduction was still similar for both groups. Women may thus still be left with a large uterine volume but without symptoms. This must be taken into consideration when counselling women with an extremely large uterus for UAE."
        }
      ]},
